## What (Stack & Structure)

**Stack**: Tauri {{TAURI_VERSION}} (Rust core, `rust-version` >= 1.90), web frontend bundled by Vite (framework of your choice), `@tauri-apps/api` {{TAURI_VERSION}}

### Project Structure
```
package.json            # Frontend deps and `tauri` script (@tauri-apps/cli)
src/                    # Frontend (any framework, bundled by Vite)
src-tauri/
├── Cargo.toml          # Rust crate (tauri, tauri-build, plugins)
├── build.rs            # tauri-build
├── tauri.conf.json     # App config: windows, bundle, security (CSP, capabilities)
├── capabilities/*.json # Permissions granted per window / role
├── icons/              # App icons
├── gen/schemas/        # Generated schemas (do not edit)
└── src/
    ├── main.rs         # Thin entry point calling `app_lib::run()`
    └── lib.rs          # `run()` with `#[cfg_attr(mobile, tauri::mobile_entry_point)]`
```

## Why (Purpose & Organization)

### Architecture: Rust core + web frontend, bridged by explicit IPC
- **Rust core**: business logic, OS access, state; exposed only through `#[tauri::command]`
- **Frontend**: UI only; talks to the core via `invoke()`, events and `Channel`
- **Capabilities**: the frontend can call only what a capability grants (least privilege)
- Keep commands thin and move logic into plain Rust modules testable without a window
- UI code: see the React / Vue / Svelte / Vite stacks; general Rust: see the `rust` stack

See `rules/02-architecture-tauri.md` for complete patterns.

### Critical Rules
| Rule | Reason |
|------|--------|
| One capability per window / role, minimal permissions | Limits what a compromised webview can do |
| Strict CSP in `app.security.csp`, Isolation pattern when relevant | XSS must not become native code access |
| Typed command errors (`thiserror` + `Serialize`) | No stringly-typed errors across the IPC boundary |
| Scoped `fs` / `http` permissions, `deny` over `allow` | Prevents path and URL abuse |
| Updater signing key kept secret, never committed | Losing it means no more updates are possible |
| `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` + `cargo fmt --check` | Code quality and consistent style |
| 80%+ coverage, IPC mocked in frontend tests | Quality assurance |

## How (Commands & Workflows)

### Development Commands
```bash
docker compose exec app npm run tauri dev            # Dev mode (Vite + Rust)
docker compose exec app npm run tauri build          # Release build and bundles
docker compose exec app cargo test --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml
docker compose exec app npx tauri add <plugin>       # Add an official plugin
docker compose exec app npx tauri signer generate -w ~/.tauri/myapp.key
```

### Quality Pipeline
```bash
docker compose exec app cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --check
docker compose exec app cargo clippy --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml -- -D warnings
docker compose exec app cargo test --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml
docker compose exec app cargo deny check             # or: cargo audit
docker compose exec app npx tsc --noEmit             # Frontend types
docker compose exec app npx vitest run               # Frontend tests (mockIPC)
```

> Desktop GUI builds need system WebView libraries; if the container cannot run them, build in CI and keep unit tests container-friendly.

### Available Commands
- `/tauri:check <architecture|code-quality|security|testing|all>` - Audit by axis, or scored aggregate

### Development Workflow
1. **Analyze** - Read code, understand domain (`rules/01-workflow-analysis.md`)
2. **TDD** - Write test -> Implement -> Refactor
3. **Quality** - Run the quality pipeline
4. **Commit** - Conventional Commits format

